# English editorial revision; retain source-page evidence and bilingual content.
E={13:[
'Wang Cong, Wu Tao and Bao Jinsong, Journal of Manufacturing Systems 84 (2026), pp. 100–116. Cognitive agents and virtual–real co-evolution address static Twins, ambiguous fault reasoning and resource coordination in industrial energy maintenance. The authors state contributions rather than numbered hypotheses.',
'A central agentic Twin orchestrates monitoring, diagnostic, decision and execution agents. Hybrid symbolic–neural representations combine thermodynamic and ontology knowledge with sensor dynamics. Memory distinguishes long-term, short-term and working contexts. Algorithms 1–2 describe memory-driven and goal/constraint-based task planning; execution outcomes feed back into memory.',
'Algorithm 3 introduces Q-learning MARL for maintenance resource decisions. Boiler states discretize flue-gas temperature, steam pressure and water-flow indicators. Actions include no action, fuel–air adjustment, soot blowing and emergency shutdown. Reward balances efficiency, safety, downtime and cost. Illustrative model families in the equations should not all be treated as independently trained experimental components.',
'The case is a gas–steam boiler in a CHP plant with five years of operation and 5.2 million historical records. An 18-month tracking study and 1,000 maintenance-cycle analyses compare rule-based expert systems, pure machine learning, static Twins and centralized or single-agent maintenance against cognitive collaboration. The paper does not provide a public benchmark split or an LLM configuration sufficient for exact reproduction.',
'The paper reports diagnostic accuracy of 97.3%, model updating reduced from 48 to 2.3 hours, fault-warning lead time extended from 24 to 72 hours and maintenance cost efficiency improved by 31.2%. Table 5 reports overall collaborative performance increasing from 82.2% to 95.5%. These are industrial case results, rather than a residential energy-control benchmark.',
'Across 18 months, diagnostic accuracy increases from 89.2% to 97.3%; knowledge entities grow from 8,427 to 15,863 and relations from 12,546 to 28,991; the calibration cycle falls from 30 to 5.8 days. The text describes 96.8% versus 78.5% collaborative diagnosis as an 18.3% improvement, whereas the table reports +23.3%; retain the reported distinction between percentage points and relative change.',
'The authors identify data quality, Twin fidelity, scalability and interpretability as limitations. Public raw data, executable component configurations and a sufficiently detailed split protocol are absent from the local article. This constrains reproduction and comparison with other domains.'
],14:[
'Barricelli et al. present a Green Smart Home (GSH) platform combining a smart-home Digital Twin, conversational LLM interaction and automation checking. The research concerns human-centered energy awareness and usable automation; it does not state a formal numerical causal-diagnosis hypothesis.',
'The three-layer architecture links Home Assistant and IoT devices to backend services and a web frontend. FastAPI, PyMongo and a TensorFlow LSTM support data management and a 12-hour consumption forecast. Natural-language requests generate structured automation descriptions rather than directly bypassing backend checks.',
'A Rule Checker tests direct and indirect conflicts, including chains of environmental effects described through semantic JSON device models. An Energy Checker evaluates power and stated duration using minute-level blocks. Possible indirect conflicts are presented as possibilities; the LLM explains problems and suggests alternatives.',
'The design evaluation includes a laboratory study with 10 users aged 23–56 completing six tasks and answering the BUS questionnaire. A subsequent study ran in six design-team members’ homes between 24 July and 20 October 2025, with variable participation periods, 50 diary entries and 29 comments. There is no randomized controller or measured energy-saving baseline.',
'Of 34 automation-creation entries, 17 reported no rule conflict, 16 reported a conflict and one was uncertain; there was one energy-overload warning. Consumption checking appears in 28 of the 50 diary entries. The evaluation supports interaction and usability findings; it does not quantify controlled energy savings or fault-recovery performance.',
'The authors discuss a small and potentially biased participant sample, heterogeneous home configurations, technical integration demands and limitations of semantic conflict modeling. Longitudinal feedback from the design team should be distinguished from an independent field trial with controlled energy outcomes.'
],17:[
'Lu et al., iScience 28 (2025), article 113867. Data2BEM automates building energy modeling from heterogeneous documents, sensor data and user instructions through calibration and retrofit assessment. The stated gap is fragmented geometry, parameterization, calibration and assessment tools. No numbered hypothesis list is presented.',
'GPT-4-based Information Retriever, Programmer, Result Analyzer, Interpreter and Reviewer agents exchange structured information and code. CAD/DXF geometry, specifications, schedules and sensor data become JSON, OpenStudio code and an EnergyPlus model. Human review remains part of the workflow.',
'Calibration uses 2018 heating measurements and a genetic algorithm with population 100, 20 generations, crossover probability 0.5 and mutation probability 0.2. Parameters and bounds are retrieved or confirmed before model runs. External numerical simulation evaluates candidates; the language model does not replace the building physics solver.',
'The real-building case evaluates geometry, heating-system modeling, schedules, simulation configuration, calibration and a boiler-to-air-source-heat-pump retrofit. Two modelers with six months and four years of experience require 32 and 8 hours, respectively, versus 48 minutes for Data2BEM. Prompt-style perturbations are tested; detailed supplementary traces are referenced but not included in the local PDF.',
'Calibration reports NMBE 2.91%, CV-RMSE 0.139 and R² 0.972. The simulated retrofit reduces energy cost from 16,164.25 to 9,026.32 (44.16%) and carbon tax from 7,813.19 to 1,621.49. These are model-based retrofit estimates, not measured savings after installation.',
'Validation concerns one richly documented building. Detailed room control and continuous updates are not tested; calibration is offline, the genetic algorithm is not universally suitable and peak-load discrepancies remain. The paper reports code at Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.17374005, while operational and design data are private. Real-time Twin adaptation remains future work.'
]}
RE={13:[
'Agentic diagnostic maintenance and adaptive Twins already have an industrial precedent. This paper matters for our novelty boundaries and for engineering memory across diagnostic episodes.',
'It contributes a separation of monitoring, diagnosis, planning and execution; memory structures; and an industrial longitudinal evaluation of adaptive maintenance.',
'Adapt the memory and task interfaces in our Operational Twin. Record evidence, interventions and outcomes with versioned parameters; keep the Reference Twin frozen for comparison. This employment is a project design recommendation, not a result tested by the article.',
'Use as an architectural helper and novelty-collision reference. A memory-disabled ablation is a candidate; reproducing the entire industrial MARL maintenance system is not a mandatory residential baseline.'
],14:[
'It demonstrates an existing DT-plus-LLM smart-home integration and identifies practical preference clarification and automation-conflict problems relevant to our research.',
'It contributes rule and energy checks, understandable alternative actions and observed interaction failures in actual homes.',
'Adapt clarification dialogues, conflict explanations and visible approval states. Our execution contracts would additionally enforce numerical physical bounds and diagnostic evidence; that extension is our design judgment.',
'Use as a helper and novelty-collision reference. An adapted rule-conflict checker is a candidate for coordination scenarios. Qualitative home feedback is not directly comparable with our recovery metrics; full replication is not mandatory.'
],17:[
'Agentic generation and calibration of building models already exist. This is relevant to Twin-update engineering and to defining what our causal intervention contribution adds.',
'It contributes structured retrieval, coding and review interfaces plus an external simulation and calibration workflow.',
'Adapt versioned calibration proposals and a reviewer interface in the isolated Operational Twin sandbox. Keep the Reference Twin frozen and require diagnostic evidence before accepting parameter changes. This is our proposed use, not an evaluated article result.',
'Use as a modeling and calibration helper rather than a mandatory control or diagnosis baseline. A calibration-workflow comparison is optional if input data and task scope can be matched.'
]}
